<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌌 LIVE OVATION AURORA FORECAST</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/aurora/legend.png?v=ovation-2026-10-09T05-19-00Z-fcst-2026-10-09T06-36-00Z" width="600" alt="legend"><br>OVATION auroral intensity, FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;30+: <b>LOWEST 0</b> (transparent, no activity) &rarr; <b>6 bright green</b> (auroral oval overhead) &rarr; <b>14 orange</b> &rarr; <b>23 magenta</b> (severe) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 30+</b> (violet extreme). Same intensity always shows the same color. Model-derived viewline in basin: <b>~48°N (at/inside this map — northern Great Lakes may see aurora low on the northern horizon when dark)</b> (southernmost latitude reaching intensity 5+: aurora may be visible low on the northern horizon from there under dark, clear skies).</p><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT FORECAST (OVATION Prime, hourly check)</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/SWPC OVATION Prime aurora model (Aurora Viewline product family)<br/><a href="https://www.spaceweather.gov/products/aurora-viewline-tonight-and-tomorrow-night-experimental">https://www.spaceweather.gov/products/aurora-viewline-tonight-and-tomorrow-night-experimental</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE OVATION AURORA FORECAST</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/aurora/legend.png?v=ovation-2026-10-09T05-19-00Z-fcst-2026-10-09T06-36-00Z" width="600" alt="Aurora gradient key"></p><p>Expected auroral intensity and oval location over the Great Lakes from NOAA's OVATION Prime model — the same model family behind NOAA's Aurora Viewline product. Colors follow NOAA's visual concept: green auroral-oval band, warming through yellow/orange to red/magenta/violet as predicted intensity rises. This is a Google Earth raster implementation using NOAA's authoritative data, not a copy of NOAA's webpage image.</p><h2>Source / Study</h2><p><b>Product:</b> NOAA/SWPC Aurora Viewline product family (experimental viewline page).<br/><b>Model:</b> OVATION Prime (real-time auroral precipitation model driven by solar-wind observations; forecast position/intensity of the auroral oval).<br/><b>Endpoint:</b> <a href="https://services.swpc.noaa.gov/json/ovation_aurora_latest.json">https://services.swpc.noaa.gov/json/ovation_aurora_latest.json</a><br/><b>Run context:</b> hemispheric power 37.9 GW; forecast Kp 4.<br/><b>Observation time:</b> 2026-10-09 01:19 AM EDT<br/><b>Forecast time:</b> 2026-10-09 02:36 AM EDT</p><h2>How to Read the Gradient</h2><p>OVATION auroral intensity, FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;30+: <b>LOWEST 0</b> (transparent, no activity) &rarr; <b>6 bright green</b> (auroral oval overhead) &rarr; <b>14 orange</b> &rarr; <b>23 magenta</b> (severe) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 30+</b> (violet extreme). Same intensity always shows the same color. Model-derived viewline in basin: <b>~48°N (at/inside this map — northern Great Lakes may see aurora low on the northern horizon when dark)</b> (southernmost latitude reaching intensity 5+: aurora may be visible low on the northern horizon from there under dark, clear skies).</p><p><b>OVATION role:</b> OVATION converts upstream solar-wind energy into maps of auroral particle precipitation; brighter regions = stronger predicted aurora overhead.</p><p><b>Viewline meaning:</b> the viewline is the southernmost region from which aurora may be seen low on the northern horizon under appropriate conditions (dark, clear, unobstructed northern view). Model-derived basin viewline now: <b>~48°N (at/inside this map — northern Great Lakes may see aurora low on the northern horizon when dark)</b>. Being south of the line means aurora is unlikely; being near or north of it means look north when dark — it is an opportunity boundary, not a sighting line.</p><p><b>Great Lakes reading:</b> green over your lake = oval possibly overhead/near; yellow→red = strong activity nearby; violet = extreme storm conditions. Daylight, clouds, moonlight and city lights can hide even a strong forecast.</p><h2>Data / Scientific Limitations</h2><p>Auroral forecast/prediction only — it does not guarantee anyone will see aurora from a specific place at a specific time. Intensity is model output in OVATION units, not a naked-eye brightness meter. Coverage is the fixed project rectangle (lon −93…−73.5, lat 40.5…49.5, WGS84, 1800×1175), inherited from LIVE LEAF COLOR; it is not cropped to shorelines. Kp context supplements but never replaces the OVATION field.</p><h2>Update Frequency</h2><p>Source checked hourly (refreshInterval 3600 s on both the entry NetworkLink and the overlay Icon). SWPC issues new OVATION runs about every 5 minutes; the layer rebuilds only when the Observation/Forecast time actually changes (source version <b>ovation-2026-10-09T05:19:00Z-fcst-2026-10-09T06:36:00Z</b>).</p>]]></description>
      <GroundOverlay>
        <name>🌌 LIVE OVATION AURORA FORECAST</name>
        <drawOrder>10</drawOrder>
        <Icon>
          <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/aurora/current.png?v=ovation-2026-10-09T05-19-00Z-fcst-2026-10-09T06-36-00Z</href>
          <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
          <refreshInterval>3600</refreshInterval>
        </Icon>
        <LatLonBox>
          <north>49.5</north>
          <south>40.5</south>
          <east>-73.5</east>
          <west>-93.0</west>
        </LatLonBox>
      </GroundOverlay>
    </Folder>
